The CSS float property is used to specify how an element should be positioned within its parent container. When an element is floated, it is taken out of the normal flow of the document and moved to the left or right side of its container. This allows other content to flow around it.
The clear property is used to specify whether an element should be positioned below, above, or next to floated elements. The clearfix property is a hack used to clear floated elements without adding additional markup to the HTML.
Here is an example of how these properties can be used in an HTML page:
```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<style>
		#container {
			border: 1px solid black;
			overflow: auto;
		}
		.box {
			float: left;
			width: 100px;
			height: 100px;
			margin: 10px;
			background-color: blue;
		}
		.clear {
			clear: both;
		}
		.clearfix::after {
			content: "";
			display: table;
			clear: both;
		}
	</style>
</head>
<body>
	<h1>Float and Clear Example</h1>
	<div id="container">
		<div class="box"></div>
		<div class="box"></div>
		<div class="box"></div>
		<div class="box"></div>
		<div class="clear"></div>
	</div>
	<div class="clearfix">
		<p>This is some text outside of the container.</p>
	</div>
</body>
</html>
```
In this example, we have a container with four boxes floated to the left. The clear class is used to add a non-floated element that clears the floats and ensures that content after the container is not affected by the floats. The clearfix class is used to clear the floats without adding an additional element to the HTML.

