There are several practical ways to implement k-means clustering in Java, including using libraries like Weka or Apache Mahout. The implementation of k-means clustering differs from supervised learning algorithms in that it does not require labeled data for training. Instead, it groups unlabeled data into clusters based on their similarity.
The potential impact of implementing k-means clustering in Java is significant, as it can be used for a variety of applications, such as customer segmentation, image compression, and anomaly detection. By clustering similar data points, k-means can help identify patterns and trends that may not be immediately apparent.
Here is a sample Java code for k-means clustering using the sum of squared errors formula:
```
public class KMeans {
    private int k;
    private int maxIterations;
    private List<Point> points;
    private List<Cluster> clusters;
    public KMeans(int k, int maxIterations, List<Point> points) {
        this.k = k;
        this.maxIterations = maxIterations;
        this.points = points;
        this.clusters = new ArrayList<>();
    }
    public void run() {
        // Initialize clusters
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            Cluster cluster = new Cluster(i);
            Point centroid = points.get(i);
            cluster.setCentroid(centroid);
            clusters.add(cluster);
        }
        // Run iterations
        for (int i = 0; i < maxIterations; i++) {
            // Assign points to clusters
            for (Point point : points) {
                Cluster closestCluster = null;
                double closestDistance = Double.MAX_VALUE;
                for (Cluster cluster : clusters) {
                    double distance = point.distanceTo(cluster.getCentroid());
                    if (distance < closestDistance) {
                        closestDistance = distance;
                        closestCluster = cluster;
                    }
                }
                closestCluster.addPoint(point);
            }
            // Update centroids of clusters
            for (Cluster cluster : clusters) {
                cluster.updateCentroid();
            }
        }
        // Print sum of squared errors
        double sumOfSquaredErrors = 0;
        for (Cluster cluster : clusters) {
            for (Point point : cluster.getPoints()) {
                double distance = point.distanceTo(cluster.getCentroid());
                sumOfSquaredErrors += Math.pow(distance, 2);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Sum of squared errors: " + sumOfSquaredErrors);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Point> points = new ArrayList<>();
        points.add(new Point(1, 2));
        points.add(new Point(2, 1));
        points.add(new Point(4, 5));
        points.add(new Point(5, 4));
        KMeans kMeans = new KMeans(2, 10, points);
        kMeans.run();
    }
}
class Point {
    private double x;
    private double y;
    public Point(double x, double y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }
    public double distanceTo(Point other) {
        double dx = x - other.x;
        double dy = y - other.y;
        return Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
    }
}
class Cluster {
    private int id;
    private Point centroid;
    private List<Point> points;
    public Cluster(int id) {
        this.id = id;
        this.points = new ArrayList<>();
    }
    public void addPoint(Point point) {
        points.add(point);
    }
    public void updateCentroid() {
        double sumX = 0;
        double sumY = 0;
        for (Point point : points) {
            sumX += point.getX();
            sumY += point.getY();
        }
        centroid = new Point(sumX / points.size(), sumY / points.size());
    }
    public Point getCentroid() {
        return centroid;
    }
    public List<Point> getPoints() {
        return points;
    }
}
```
To optimize the algorithm's parameters, we can use a multi-step reasoning process:
1. Vary the number of clusters (k) and the maximum number of iterations to find the optimal values that minimize the sum of squared errors.
2. Use elbow method to determine the optimal value of k by plotting sum of squared errors against k and selecting the value at which the rate of decrease sharply shifts.
3. Use silhouette analysis to evaluate the quality of clustering by calculating the average silhouette score for each value of k and selecting the value with the highest average score.
4. Repeat the above steps with different distance metrics and initialization methods to find the optimal combination of parameters that yield the best clustering performance.

