How can I use C# code to solve this milk-drinking riddle? 
There are three kids named John, Emily, and Tom. Each of them drank a different amount of milk, measured in glasses. Here's what we know:
- The total number of glasses they drank is 6.
- Tom drank twice as much milk as Emily.
- John drank one more glass than Emily.
Can you provide a C# code solution using a class and LINQ to determine how many glasses of milk each kid drank? The class 'MilkDrinking' should have the following properties: 'Name' (string), 'GlassesDrunk' (int). Don't forget to use the plural form of "glass" in your output statements!
Example output:
John drank 2 glasses of milk.
Emily drank 1 glass of milk.
Tom drank 3 glasses of milk.
[C# code]
public class MilkDrinking
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int GlassesDrunk { get; set; }
}
var kids = new List<MilkDrinking>
{
    new MilkDrinking { Name = "John" },
    new MilkDrinking { Name = "Emily" },
    new MilkDrinking { Name = "Tom" }
};
var combinations = from j in Enumerable.Range(0, 4)
                   from e in Enumerable.Range(0, 4 - j)
                   from t in Enumerable.Range(0, 4 - j - e)
                   where j + e + t == 6 && j == e + 1 && t == e * 2
                   select new { John = j, Emily = e, Tom = t };
foreach (var c in combinations)
{
    kids[0].GlassesDrunk = c.John;
    kids[1].GlassesDrunk = c.Emily;
    kids[2].GlassesDrunk = c.Tom;
    Console.WriteLine("{0} drank {1} glass{2} of milk.", kids[0].Name, kids[0].GlassesDrunk, kids[0].GlassesDrunk == 1 ? "" : "es");
    Console.WriteLine("{0} drank {1} glass{2} of milk.", kids[1].Name, kids[1].GlassesDrunk, kids[1].GlassesDrunk == 1 ? "" : "es");
    Console.WriteLine("{0} drank {1} glass{2} of milk.", kids[2].Name, kids[2].GlassesDrunk, kids[2].GlassesDrunk == 1 ? "" : "es");
}

